e42e4aa55edf918961e952ff3","download_count":0,"created_at":"2026-08-16T23:20:18Z","updated_at":"2026-08-16T23:20:19Z","browser_download_url":"https://github.com/J-Tech-Japan/Sekiban/releases/download/dcb-v10.15.0/Sekiban.Dcb.WithResult.Testing.10.15.0.nupkg"}],"tarball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/J-Tech-Japan/Sekiban/tarball/dcb-v10.15.0","zipball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/J-Tech-Japan/Sekiban/zipball/dcb-v10.15.0","body":"## Materialized views: forbidden transitions now fail closed, and a new verified-execution mode\n\nResolves #1130 and #1129.\n\n### VerifyOnly no longer returns an apparent success (#1130)\n\n`MvInitializationMode.VerifyOnly` correctly performed no writes, but calling a mutating operation while it was active returned integer zero — indistinguishable from \"there were no applicable events\". Automation could continue past an unsupported operation believing work had simply been empty.\n\nEvery operation whose advertised postcondition is a mutation now refuses with a typed error **before** any event-store, host, inspector, or writable-database work: event apply, catch-up (including an empty batch), target checkpoint capture, lifecycle promotion and status update, activation, forced reverse, and refresh. Read-only operations — schema verification, read-only registry and active-pointer inspection, plain status reads — remain legitimate no-ops.\n\nThe new `MvTransitionNotAllowedException` carries the mode, the attempted transition, a typed reason and the view identity, and is distinct from schema mismatch, provider-capability failure, SQL-policy denial, cancellation, and a genuine zero-event result. Result-shaped APIs gain `MvActivationFailureReason.TransitionNotAllowed` instead of reusing the generic provider failure. Method signatures are unchanged.\n\n### New mode: run a materialized view without granting DDL (#1129)\n\n`VerifyAndExecute` (mode value 2) verifies the pre-provisioned schema exactly as `VerifyOnly` does, and then runs the ordinary materialized-view lifecycle:\n\n- projector DML executes only after the host's SQL statement policy authorizes the whole batch;\n- checkpoint, lifecycle status and active-pointer transitions (including first activation and forward/reverse switching) proceed through the normal DCB contracts, so progress and lifecycle truth are reported natively;\n- infrastructure ensure, projector initialization SQL and registry seeding remain forbidden, exactly as in `VerifyOnly`.\n\nThis mode is explicitly **not** side-effect free: writing rows into pre-provisioned tables is DML, not DDL. It requires `Enforced` policy mode plus an explicitly supplied policy — a missing, null or allow-all policy fails with a typed configuration error before any database or host call — and it is designed to run under a database principal that holds the required table DML grants and no CREATE/ALTER/DROP authority.\n\nAcceptance was proven against a real PostgreSQL instance using such a DDL-denied role, with negative controls showing CREATE, ALTER and DROP each fail for that role, command recording on every Sekiban-owned connection, and before/after schema fingerprints. The allow case commits exactly one row and advances the checkpoint exactly once with zero DDL; deny-all and mixed allow/deny batches leave rows, checkpoints, status and the active pointer byte-for-byte unchanged.\n\n### Internals\n\nAll three modes are now decided by one exhaustive capability mapping rather than scattered boolean checks, and an unknown numeric mode value fails closed before any database or host call.\n\n### Compatibility\n\n- Additive only: mode values 0 and 1, all existing CLR signatures, and every default are unchanged; no wire, schema or storage change and no data migration.\n- Verified by a consumer compiled against the published 10.14.1 packages and executed against the new assemblies without recompilation, plus a new consumer covering mode value 2 and the typed refusal.\n- **Migration note:** callers that previously invoked a mutating materialized-view operation under `VerifyOnly` and treated the returned zero as success will now receive `MvTransitionNotAllowedException`. That is the intended correction; switch such callers to `VerifyAndExecute` if they meant to execute, or stop issuing the call in inspection mode.\n\nDetails: #1131 (PR #1132), reported as #1130 and #1129.\n"}